      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "While the mathematical formulation of TemporalTrans appears theoretically sound, the empirical evidence regarding long-range dependency modeling remains insufficient, particularly under high-noise conditions typical in real-world data. The authors claim a significant linear scaling advantage, yet omitting comparisons with established baselines on extended sequences makes these findings appear contradictory to current benchmarks where linear complexity is already the norm for efficient sampling. Consequently, the limitations of this architecture are not clearly defined given the lack of failure mode analysis, and the proposed adaptive attention mechanism would benefit from a more rigorous validation strategy against standard time-series feature engineering. Additionally, the reliance solely on binary classification metrics like MSE overlooks how the method degrades on multi-step forecasting tasks, suggesting that the novelty of the mechanism is overstated. A better approach would address these oversights by including ablation studies on sequence length robustness and sensitivity analysis before concluding on superior performance. Furthermore, the experimental design fails to account for data leakage, which renders some efficiency claims potentially flawed when applied to unseen temporal patterns. Finally, the omission of computational overhead analysis contradicts the implied trade-off between accuracy and latency inherent in transformer-based solutions.",
      "source": "target",
